# Backend Rules

## Technology

- Use Java 21.
- Follow the existing Spring Boot version.
- Follow current project conventions.
- Use Maven and the existing Maven Wrapper.
- Do not add a dependency without explaining why it is required.
- Do not replace an existing dependency without approval.

## Layering

- Controllers must remain thin.
- Controllers must not contain business calculations.
- Controllers must not contain matching rules.
- Controllers must not contain CBT state transitions.
- Services own business logic.
- Services own transaction boundaries.
- Repositories only access persistence.
- Do not expose JPA entities directly through REST APIs.
- Use explicit request DTOs.
- Use explicit response DTOs.
- Mappers only perform mapping.
- Mappers must not contain business rules.

## Validation

- Validate external input using Bean Validation where appropriate.
- Re-check business invariants inside the service.
- Reject invalid enum values.
- Reject access to resources owned by another user.
- Do not trust a client-provided `userId`.
- Use the authenticated principal as the current user identity.

## Error Handling

- Follow the shared ErrorResponse format in OpenAPI.
- Use stable error codes.
- Do not expose stack traces.
- Do not expose SQL details.
- Do not expose internal class names.
- Map expected business errors to appropriate HTTP status codes.

## Security

- Every user-scoped resource requires ownership verification.
- Role checks do not replace ownership checks.
- USER must not read another user's data.
- EXPERT must not automatically access all user data.
- Do not log passwords.
- Do not log JWTs.
- Do not log API keys.
- Do not log full sensitive chat content.
- Do not log full sensitive exercise submissions.
- Read secrets from environment configuration.
- Never hard-code secret values.

## Transactions

- A transaction should cover one complete local business action.
- Do not keep a database transaction open during a slow external LLM call.
- Use explicit processing statuses where appropriate:
  - PENDING
  - SUCCEEDED
  - FAILED
- Avoid hidden side effects in getters.
- Avoid hidden side effects in mappers.
- Avoid unnecessary entity lifecycle callbacks.

## API Contract

- Follow `docs/03_API_CONTRACT.yaml`.
- Do not silently rename request or response fields.
- Update OpenAPI before changing a shared endpoint contract.
- Use UTC timestamps.
- Return ISO-8601 date-time values.
- Follow the shared pagination response structure.
- Do not return internal database-only fields without a clear requirement.

## Testing

- Add unit tests for business rules.
- Add integration tests for critical REST APIs.
- Add integration tests for persistence constraints.
- Test successful access.
- Test unauthenticated access.
- Test forbidden cross-user access.
- Use Mock AI Provider in normal automated tests.
- Real LLM tests require an explicit environment flag.
- Run relevant Maven commands.
- Report the exact command and result.

## Code Quality

- Prefer explicit domain services.
- Avoid unnecessary generic managers.
- Avoid unnecessary helper classes.
- Avoid unnecessary `ServiceImpl` classes when there is only one implementation.
- Avoid reflection-heavy solutions.
- Keep methods focused.
- Do not refactor unrelated packages.
- Do not change naming conventions during an unrelated task.